================================================================================
 KNOWING EYE -- GUIDANCE EXAMINATION & AI PROCTORING SYSTEM
 CAPSTONE DEFENSE REVISION CHECKLIST
 Compiled from panel defense feedback | For team use going into Capstone 2
================================================================================

HOW TO USE THIS FILE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Organized into 5 working areas, matching the 5 major themes from the defense.
- Each item is tagged [P0] / [P1] / [P2]:
    [P0] = must fix before Capstone 2 -- these came up as direct challenges
    [P1] = important -- plan into a sprint, don't skip
    [P2] = refinement / polish -- do once P0/P1 are stable
- Check items off as [x] while you work through them.
- Section 6 has the same items flattened into one priority-ordered backlog.
- Section 7 lists what the panel already liked -- don't rebuild these.

================================================================================
 1. AUTHENTICATION, ROLES & ACCESS CONTROL
================================================================================
Panel concern: one administrator account is a bottleneck and a single point of
failure. They explicitly raised staff logins and delegated operational access.

1.1 Role-based access control [P0]
    [x] Replace the single-administrator model with real RBAC
    [x] Do not allow admin privileges to be casually shared/delegated
    [x] Define roles: Administrator, Guidance Staff, Program Head,
        Teacher/Exam Creator, Proctor, Examinee
    [x] Define permissions per role: create, read, update, delete, approve,
        monitor, manage users, manage exams, view reports
    [x] Consider permission-based access if RBAC alone isn't dynamic enough
    [x] Model access around the actual Guidance Office workflow

1.2 Administrator responsibilities [P0]
    [x] Separate administrative oversight from operational tasks
    [x] Stop making the administrator create every exam personally
    [x] Let teachers / program heads create or submit exams
    [x] Let program heads review / revise / approve submitted exams
    [x] Define a hierarchy, e.g.:
          Level 0: Administrator
          Level 1: Program Head / authorized staff
          Other: operational roles

1.3 Registration & verification [P0]
    [x] Simplify registration to essential fields only
    [x] Remove required profile photo at signup -- make it optional,
        add/change after registration
    [x] Implement OTP verification
    [x] Add email verification
    [x] Add password confirmation
    [x] Make the registration flow visually clear about where OTP happens

================================================================================
 2. UI / UX REDESIGN
================================================================================
Largest volume of feedback. Panel could not read the UI comfortably and did
not feel "Legacy College" in the design.

2.1 Readability & contrast [P0]
    [x] Increase text/background contrast app-wide
    [x] Review button, disabled-state, and input-field contrast
    [x] Eliminate gray-on-gray combinations
    [x] Make status states visually distinguishable at a glance

2.2 Tone & theme [P1]
    [x] Pull back from the current dark "night theme" look
    [x] Rebuild visual hierarchy so it reads well under real classroom light

2.3 Institutional identity [P0]
    [ ] Add Legacy College branding: colors, marks, recognizable elements
        (institution logo + campus photo done; department seal is still a
        labeled placeholder pending the office's official mark)
    [x] Make it feel like a school system, not a generic SaaS dashboard
    [x] Redesign obviously templated / AI-generated-looking components
    [x] Make every layout decision intentional and defensible

2.4 Dashboard organization [P2]
    [ ] Review placement/hierarchy of every major component
    [x] Improve sort / search / filter interfaces
    [ ] Decongest cramped views
    [x] Make status colors meaningful and consistent

================================================================================
 3. EXAM MANAGEMENT & LMS BEHAVIOR
================================================================================
Instruction from panel: "Don't reinvent the LMS examination workflow --
benchmark it."

3.1 Exam discovery [P0]
    [x] Add exam categories for filtering
    [x] Add search, filter, sort -- don't rely on cards alone at scale

3.2 Scheduling & exam codes [P0]
    [x] Add opening + closing date/time (a real examination window)
    [x] Block access after deadline
    [x] Show clear states: Upcoming / Active / Closed / Expired
    [x] Keep automatic exam-code generation (dept abbreviation + year),
        lock it from accidental edits
    [x] Keep exam title editable separately from the generated code

3.3 Department & section assignment [P1]
    [x] Allow one exam to be assigned to multiple departments (multi-select)
        for shared/general-ed exams
    [ ] Support section-level assignment and per-group schedules
    [x] Add exam cloning/duplication instead of full re-creation per group

3.4 Question management [P1]
    [x] Support reordering (drag-and-drop), editing, deleting
    [x] Lock question editing once an exam is in progress
        (students may already be answering)
    [x] Support images in questions AND in answer choices
        (abstract/psychological items need image-based options)

3.5 Bulk import [P1]
    [x] Support XLSX/CSV import with a downloadable template
    [x] Validate: question type, choices, correct answer, ordering,
        media references
    [x] Document template columns + example row + accepted question types

3.6 LMS benchmarking [P0]
    [x] Study Google Forms, Jotform, and comparable LMS platforms
    [x] Compare: question creation flow, drag-and-drop reordering,
        question types, randomization/shuffling, search/filtering,
        scheduling/publishing, question bank behavior, results workflow
    [x] Turn this into an actual comparison table in the documentation

3.7 Guidance-specific scope [P1]
    [x] Design for psychological / mental / abstract / behavioral content,
        not a generic academic quiz
    [ ] Restrict question access -- confidential content stays in
        Guidance Office (RBAC/module access already scopes exam
        management; no distinct Guidance-only content restriction beyond
        that has been built)
    [x] Reconsider whether "department" should be a primary exam attribute
        at all, since Guidance's process is broader than one department

3.8 Results [P1]
    [x] Hold essay results for manual grading (can't auto-release)
    [x] Add email notification on result release
    [ ] Give admins/proctors per-exam results, warnings, terminations,
        exportable reports (generic session reports already filter by
        exam/status and export CSV/PDF; no dedicated per-exam report yet)

================================================================================
 4. COMPUTER VISION & MONITORING
================================================================================
Most technical line of questioning. CV concept + identity matching landed
well -- but thresholds, dataset methodology, and algorithm choice need real
justification, not developer judgment calls.

  DIRECT PANEL CHALLENGE: "Are your threshold values based on research,
  or did the team just decide them?"

4.1 Define every CV parameter [P0]
    For EACH of: face presence, identity match, head-facing camera,
    upper-body visibility, head movement, eye gaze, posture -- document:
    [x] What it measures
    [x] Why it matters
    [x] What is normal vs. suspicious
    [x] What algorithm detects it
    [x] What threshold triggers a flag
    [x] What action happens when the threshold is exceeded

4.2 Threshold justification [P0]
    [x] Define "anomaly" precisely per parameter
    [x] Base thresholds on cited research, not internal judgment
        (identity threshold: InsightFace's own guide; gaze: proctoring-CV
        literature; temporal window: anomaly-detection literature; posture
        ratios documented as internally-derived, not misattributed)
    [x] Use explicit tiers: Normal -> Suspicious -> Anomalous
    [x] Factor in duration and frequency, not single-frame events
        (reduces false positives)

4.3 Anomaly accumulation model [P0]
    Replace "one head turn = anomaly" with accumulation over time/signals.
    Escalation path:  Normal -> Warning -> Suspicious -> Critical -> Action
    [x] Count repeated occurrences of the same signal
        (e.g. face missing x1, x2, x3...)
    [x] Weight combined signals higher than one isolated signal
    [x] Define what happens at each tier: notify, warn, pause,
        proctor intervention, terminate (Action tier deliberately kept
        manual -- Critical recommends intervention, a proctor still clicks
        terminate; see rationale in docs/documentation/chapter2/
        03-system-design.html)

4.4 Dataset methodology [P0]
    [x] Document data source, collection method, sample size
        (honest answer: no dataset exists yet -- MediaPipe/ArcFace run as
        frozen pretrained models, no custom training occurs; documented as
        planned methodology for the deployment window, not fabricated
        results)
    [x] Document cleaning, annotation, labeling process (planned, per above)
    [x] State and justify train/test split
        (70/15/15 stated in advance; train partition reserved/unused since
        no model is trained here)

4.5 Algorithm comparison [P0]
    [x] Benchmark current stack (MediaPipe, ArcFace/InsightFace) against
        alternatives (Dlib, OpenPose, Dlib face_recognition), using
        published literature numbers -- also corrected 4 doc pages that
        falsely claimed YOLOv8 was implemented (it never was: zero
        ultralytics dependency or code anywhere in the repo)
    [x] Compare accuracy, speed, resource cost
    [x] State why the selected algorithm best fits the requirements

4.6 Camera & hardware requirements [P0]
    Camera:
      [x] Position, angle, distance, field of view
      [x] Minimum / recommended resolution, lighting conditions
    Client device:
      [x] RAM, processor, OS, browser, camera, internet requirement
    Server:
      [x] CPU, RAM, storage, network, hosting environment,
          concurrent-user target
          (CPU/RAM figures are extrapolated from a real n=3 measurement,
          not a validated 30/100-concurrency benchmark - see 4.7)
    NOTE: "It's web-based" is not a technical requirements section --
    camera processing still has real hardware demands.
    See docs/documentation/chapter2/03-system-design.html#capacity.

4.7 Capacity & performance [P0]
    [x] Build the load-test harness (scripts/load/ - Locust, REST + the
        WebSocket snapshot channel, real ~1fps frontend capture profile)
    [ ] Establish max simultaneous examinees per room and per server
        -- NOT validated at target scale. Harness only got to run n=3 on
        this sprint's dev hardware (see below) before being intentionally
        stopped rather than risk crashing a memory-constrained shared
        machine. Re-run on dedicated hardware to get the real numbers.
    [~] Load-test at increasing concurrency: 5 -> 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 50 -> 100
        -- only n=3 actually run (twice, for harness validation); higher
        rungs blocked by <1GB free host RAM, not by the harness or the app
    [x] Measure response time, CPU, RAM, network, error rate at each step
        (done for n=3; same methodology scales to every rung once hardware
        allows it)
    [ ] Answer directly: can it handle ~30/room and ~100 total?
        -- Honest answer given real data: not established yet. See
        "Answering the Directive's question" in the design doc.

4.8 Snapshot monitoring & proctor dashboard [P1]
    [x] Document: monitoring uses periodic snapshots over WebSockets,
        NOT continuous video -- explain why (bandwidth/processing cost)
    [x] State snapshot frequency, trigger, retention policy
    [x] Give proctor a locator to find a flagged student physically
        (ExamAssignment.seat_label, set via roster CSV import, surfaced on
        the live-session card and session exports)
    [x] Fix the camera-preview widget in one screen position (no scroll drift)
        (already position:fixed in proctoring-dock.tsx -- verified, not rebuilt)

4.9 Browser-side deterrents [P2]
    [x] Disable right-click, text selection, copy where appropriate
        (never inside an answer input/textarea)
    [x] Detect tab-switching; consider fullscreen mode
        (detection + logging already existed end-to-end; added counting
        against Exam.max_tab_switches with an on-screen warning once
        exceeded. Fullscreen: not force-mandated -- browser permission
        quirks make forcing it unreliable across environments)
    [x] Document these as deterrents, NOT absolute security --
        don't overclaim in the paper

================================================================================
 5. DOCUMENTATION & RESEARCH
================================================================================
Chapters 1-2 need to stand on their own before Capstone 2 begins.

5.1 Related literature [P0]
    [ ] Add literature on facial detection/recognition, head pose,
        eye gaze, posture, behavioral anomaly detection in exam monitoring
    [ ] Cite the source for every technical claim in the paper
        (panel asked directly where claims came from)
    [ ] Chain: literature -> factor -> parameter -> algorithm ->
        measurement -> threshold, explicitly, in the writeup

5.2 Testing methodology -- MAJOR CORRECTION [P0]
    Team was treating testing as post-deployment only. Panel corrected this:
    testing runs continuously, before, during, and after deployment.

      During development:  unit, integration, functional, CV, UI,
                            performance, access/security testing
      Before deployment:   pre-deployment validation, readiness check,
                            fix critical issues
      During deployment:   gather real-world results, measure performance,
                            collect user feedback, fix live issues
      After deployment:    analyze results, document findings,
                            final revisions

5.3 Work breakdown & system design [P1]
    [ ] Rebuild WBS around Agile sprints (design -> develop -> test ->
        sprint -> repeat), not sequential phases
    [ ] Add a physical/floor-plan diagram: examinee seating, camera
        placement, proctor position, network layout
    [ ] Update use-case diagrams to reflect new RBAC roles/permissions

5.4 Deployment plan (Capstone 2) [P1]
    [ ] Complete pre-deployment validation before going live with
        the Guidance Office
    [ ] Run an actual deployment window of roughly six weeks
    [ ] Collect deployment results, log issues, fix them, document
        outcomes for the final paper

